# ai_dev_loop Global Integration Contracts

These contracts protect the user-level Codex integration layer for `ai_dev_loop`. They apply in addition to `ai-dev-loop-governance.mdc`, `ai-dev-loop-orchestrator-contracts.mdc`, `ai-dev-loop-state-and-schema-contracts.mdc`, `ai-dev-loop-codex-review-contracts.mdc`, `ai-dev-loop-loop-and-resume-contracts.mdc`, and `ai-dev-loop-abort-contracts.mdc`.

## Source Of Truth

- `archive/implementation-history/master-plan.md` is the archived master architecture contract.
- The active phase plan narrows implementation scope.
- Global integration assets are user-level Codex assets, not target-repository run state.
- If existing user files are invalid or ambiguous, fail safely and preserve diagnostics instead of overwriting them.

## Installed Paths

- Install only the package-owned skills at:
  - `$HOME/.agents/skills/ai-dev-loop-handoff/SKILL.md`
  - `$HOME/.agents/skills/ai-dev-loop-controller/SKILL.md`
  - `$HOME/.agents/skills/ai-dev-loop-fallback-recovery/SKILL.md`
  for the selected target's Codex-visible home.
- Use `OWNED_SKILLS` in `src/ai_dev_loop/integrations/codex/assets.py` as the
  package-owned resource inventory, including fallback recovery's reference and
  agent metadata files. Do not invent additional install roots.
- For `wsl-cli`, the Codex-visible home is the WSL user home.
- For `codex-desktop-wsl`, the Codex-visible home is the Windows user profile exposed to WSL (for example `/mnt/c/Users/<user>`), not the WSL home.
- Do not install skills under `~/.codex/skills` or any invented/deprecated skill path.
- Install the SessionStart hook script only at the WSL user's `$HOME/.codex/hooks/ai_dev_loop_session_start.py`. The hook script always lives in WSL even for the desktop target.
- Register the hook through the selected target's Codex-visible `hooks.json`.
- For `wsl-cli`, register under `$HOME/.codex/hooks.json` in WSL.
- For `codex-desktop-wsl`, register under the Windows Codex home `hooks.json`, but the registered command must invoke WSL explicitly via `wsl.exe`.
- Store hook session metadata under XDG-managed `ai_dev_loop` state in WSL, not under Windows application state.
- Do not write `state.json`, hook session records, prompts, reviews, patches, logs, or agent output inside target repositories.
- Do not share the entire Windows `.codex` home with WSL.
- Do not set WSL `CODEX_HOME` to `/mnt/c/.../.codex`.
- Do not symlink, copy, migrate, open, or inspect Codex SQLite databases across Windows and WSL.

## Integration Targets

- Support `wsl-cli` and `codex-desktop-wsl` explicitly via `--target`.
- Default target may remain `wsl-cli` for backward compatibility.
- `integrations install --target codex-desktop-wsl` must not silently create the desktop session bridge unless an explicit flag such as `--install-session-bridge` is supplied.
- Session bridge creation must use `integrations sessions install` or the explicit bridge flag.
- Legacy helper material such as `scripts/share-codex-desktop-sessions.sh` and `scripts/codex-desktop-wsl-sessions.md` is reference material only unless an explicit later plan converts it into documentation or a thin wrapper around the Python CLI. Do not reintroduce a loose shell script as the product interface for bridge setup.

## Desktop WSL Hook Command

- The Windows `hooks.json` command for `codex-desktop-wsl` must invoke WSL explicitly with `wsl.exe`, the selected distro, `--exec`, the configured Python command, and the WSL hook script path.
- Validate every dynamic token included in the Windows command string: WSL distro name, Python command, and hook script path.
- Quote tokens with whitespace in the generated command string.
- Reject newlines, quotes, and Windows command metacharacters in dynamic command tokens, including `&`, `|`, `<`, `>`, `^`, `%`, `!`, `(`, and `)`.
- Do not rely on shell interpretation or shell escaping to make unsafe command text safe.

## Desktop Session Bridge

- Bridge path: `<wsl-codex-home>/sessions/from-desktop` -> `<windows-codex-home>/sessions`.
- Reject WSL `CODEX_HOME` values under `/mnt/*`.
- Reject when the whole WSL `sessions/` directory is itself a symlink.
- Create only the nested `from-desktop` symlink; never symlink the entire sessions directory or whole Codex home.
- Bridge status must not treat any `from-desktop` symlink as healthy by presence alone. Resolve the symlink target and compare it with the resolved Windows desktop sessions directory.
- If `from-desktop` points anywhere other than the resolved Windows desktop sessions directory, status and doctor must warn, report the bridge as unhealthy, skip rollout counting through that link, and `integrations sessions list` must refuse to list through it.
- `integrations sessions remove` must resolve only WSL-side bridge paths. It must not require Windows home detection, because stale bridge cleanup must remain possible when Windows detection fails.
- `integrations sessions remove` removes only the bridge symlink and refuses non-symlink paths.
- `integrations uninstall` must not remove the session bridge by default.
- `integrations sessions list` may read rollout filenames only; do not read rollout contents or SQLite state.
- Retained Phase 10 compatibility code may resolve an exact UUID through the
  validated bridge solely to extract allowlisted runtime scalars. New scheduler
  submissions do not read A's rollout or require the bridge to create reviewer B.
- Session runtime extraction must not retain transcript content, arbitrary event payloads, or the absolute rollout path. Ambiguous UUID matches or untrusted roots must fail closed.

## Skill Contract

- Package-owned skill names must match `OWNED_SKILLS`: `ai-dev-loop-handoff`,
  `ai-dev-loop-controller`, and `ai-dev-loop-fallback-recovery`.
- Handoff submits an approved plan and exact prompt with explicit review model
  and reasoning. A is optional provenance; no app fork, cross-chat message, or
  pre-existing B is required. Do not pass `--codex-session-id`.
- The scheduler creates B once at the first review, then resumes its exact
  identity. Controller actions must never send prompts to or resume B directly.
- Controller commands are `controller status`, `scheduler start`,
  `scheduler abort`, and read-only scheduler status/list/history/timeline.
  Refuse controller actions from the run's bound reviewer identity. Known run ID
  and repository suffice for lookup; missing optional A metadata does not block it.
- If A provenance is supplied, use the exact known SessionStart ID. Never use
  `--last`, infer IDs, or make session discovery a new-run prerequisite.
- The handoff skill must tell Codex to read the target repository's `ai_dev_loop.yaml`.
- Do not restore retired top-level `start`/`launch` or session-bound preparation.
- The skills must not hard-code project names, plan paths, prompt paths, model names,
  or review skill names.
- Fallback recovery is a separately authorized agent workflow that reproduces a
  stable staged implementation in an isolated worktree and submits a fresh
  ordinary run. It is not `scheduler review retry`, does not preserve B, and is
  not a new `scheduler recovery` command. Follow its explicit admission/scope
  limits; do not trigger it automatically for every failed review.

## SessionStart Hook Contract

- The hook script must be standalone and standard-library-only after installation.
- The hook must read one JSON object from stdin and return valid JSON on stdout.
- Accept only SessionStart sources `startup`, `resume`, `clear`, and `compact`.
- Extract only minimal metadata: session ID, model, cwd, transcript path string if supplied, source, and timestamp.
- Validate any externally supplied value before using it as a filesystem path component.
- Treat `session_id` as untrusted hook input. It must pass a strict safe format check, such as UUID format or an equally narrow allowlist, before being used in a filename or returned in additional context.
- Reject `session_id` values containing path separators, traversal components such as `..`, whitespace-only content, or any character outside the accepted format.
- Never read, copy, parse, summarize, or persist transcript contents.
- Never store authentication files, API keys, tokens, full process environments, full prompts, patches, reviews, or agent output.
- If input is invalid or incomplete, exit safely with minimal valid JSON and no destructive side effects.
- The additional context must clearly state the exact current Codex session ID and must warn not to infer another session or use `--last`.

## Hook Registration Contract

- Preserve unrelated top-level keys in `hooks.json`.
- Preserve unrelated hook events and unrelated `SessionStart` entries.
- Merge idempotently and avoid duplicate `ai_dev_loop` hook command entries.
- Validate existing JSON before writing. If it is invalid, fail without overwriting it.
- Back up an existing `hooks.json` before modifying it.
- Write `hooks.json` atomically.
- Use deterministic command identity so uninstall can remove only the `ai_dev_loop` hook entry.
- Never identify owned hooks by substring matching alone.
- Uninstall must remove only an exact expected command or another explicitly documented ownership marker that cannot match unrelated hooks accidentally.
- Install may clean stale `ai_dev_loop` hook registrations only when a strong ownership signal is present, such as the expected status message plus the canonical `ai_dev_loop` hook path shape. Same-named scripts without the ownership signal must be preserved.
- Do not automatically trust hooks or edit Codex trust state.
- Install output must tell the user to open `/hooks` in Codex and trust the `ai_dev_loop` hook.
- Install output must state that the hook may be skipped until trusted.

## Uninstall Contract

- Remove only assets installed by `ai_dev_loop`.
- Preserve unrelated skills and hook scripts.
- Preserve unrelated hook registrations.
- Validate and parse `hooks.json` before deleting installed files. If `hooks.json` is invalid, leave the skill file, hook script, and hook registration untouched and report the validation error.
- Do all fallible validation work before destructive or irreversible file operations whenever mutating user-global files.
- Delete only package-owned resources from the current inventory and the owned
  hook script. Remove installed directories only when empty afterward.
- Preserve user-added files inside package-owned skill directories
  (`ai-dev-loop-handoff`, `ai-dev-loop-controller`, and
  `ai-dev-loop-fallback-recovery`).
- Preserve run history, prompts, reviews, staged patches, logs, XDG state, and XDG config by default.
- Back up `hooks.json` before removing the `ai_dev_loop` hook registration.
- If `hooks.json` is invalid, fail safely and do not rewrite it.
- Uninstall does not perform state cleanup. The existing explicit
  `scheduler cutover cleanup` has a separate confirmation and ownership boundary.

## Status And Doctor

- `integrations status` must be read-only.
- `doctor` must remain read-only.
- Report exact installed paths, parse status, registration status, package-content match status, and duplicate registrations when detected.
- Report hook trust as `unknown` when it cannot be safely detected.
- Do not print full `hooks.json`, full session IDs, transcript contents, prompts, reviews, patches, raw JSONL, auth payloads, or secret-like values in default output.
- JSON output should be stable and machine-readable.

## Permissions And Packaging

- Use package resources for installable assets, not working-tree-relative paths.
- Ensure assets are included in wheel and sdist builds.
- Apply user-only permissions where supported: directories `0700`; files containing session IDs, transcript paths, hook script content, skill instructions, or hook config `0600`.
- When creating hook-owned XDG state paths, apply `0700` to the `ai_dev_loop` state root and every managed subdirectory, not only the final leaf directory.
- Avoid rewriting installed files that already match the current package content.

## Tests

- Automated tests must use temporary `HOME` and XDG directories.
- Desktop bridge and desktop integration tests must use hermetic native-WSL temp/home/Codex env fixtures. Do not let `tmp_path`, `TMP`, `TEMP`, `TMPDIR`, or inherited `CODEX_HOME` accidentally place the fake WSL Codex home under `/mnt/*`.
- Add explicit coverage for Windows-propagated WSL environments where `TMP`, `TEMP`, `TMPDIR`, or `CODEX_HOME` point under `/mnt/*`, and prove the product either rejects unsafe runtime configuration or uses explicit native WSL overrides in tests.
- When full-suite pytest capture is incompatible with DrvFS temp directories before fixtures can run, run full-suite validation with native WSL temp such as `TMPDIR=/tmp TMP=/tmp TEMP=/tmp`; use focused `-s` runs for DrvFS propagation simulations.
- Do not touch the real user's `~/.agents`, `~/.codex`, or XDG state in tests.
- Do not invoke real Cursor or Codex model activity in tests.
- Test first-time install.
- Test idempotent install.
- Test install with existing unrelated hooks.
- Test invalid `hooks.json` fail-safe behavior.
- Test uninstall preserving unrelated hooks.
- Test hook input/output and minimal session metadata persistence.
- Test status JSON and doctor checks.
- Test package build includes integration assets.
- Test target-aware install/status/uninstall for `wsl-cli` and `codex-desktop-wsl`.
- Test desktop hook command generation uses `wsl.exe`, distro, `--exec`, and the WSL hook script path.
- Test desktop hook command generation quotes tokens with spaces and rejects unsafe command-token characters for distro, Python command, and hook script path.
- Test desktop install does not create the session bridge unless explicitly requested.
- Test `integrations sessions install/status/list/remove` with temporary fixture homes.
- Test bridge install rejects WSL `CODEX_HOME` under `/mnt/*` and whole-sessions symlinks.
- Test bridge status warns on a mispointed `from-desktop` symlink and treats it as unhealthy.
- Test `integrations sessions list` refuses a mispointed bridge.
- Test bridge removal works without Windows home detection.
- Test desktop uninstall preserves unrelated Windows hooks and the session bridge.
- Test invalid Windows `hooks.json` fail-safe behavior for the desktop target.
- Test doctor/status JSON includes desktop target and bridge checks without mutation.
- Test unsafe `session_id` values, including path traversal and non-UUID values, do not write session files or return session context.
- Test invalid `hooks.json` during uninstall preserves installed files and does not leave a broken registration pointing at a missing hook script.
- Test install and uninstall preserve unrelated hooks that reference a same-named hook script outside the owned command identity.
- Test install preserves unrelated same-named canonical hooks when the `ai_dev_loop` ownership signal is absent.
- Test uninstall preserves user-added files in the handoff skill directory.
- Test hook-created XDG parent directories and leaf directories use `0700` where the filesystem supports chmod.

## Review Regression Tests

These regressions came from Phase 7 staged reviews and must remain covered when global integration code changes:

- unsafe `session_id` must not allow path traversal outside `codex-sessions`;
- invalid `hooks.json` during uninstall must not delete `SKILL.md` or the installed hook script;
- install must preserve unrelated same-named hooks unless they carry the documented `ai_dev_loop` ownership signal;
- uninstall must preserve unrelated same-named hooks by using exact ownership identity;
- uninstall must preserve user files inside package-owned skill directories
  (`ai-dev-loop-handoff` and `ai-dev-loop-controller`);
- first install must create both owned `SKILL.md` files; uninstall must remove both
  owned `SKILL.md` files when `hooks.json` validation succeeds;
- SessionStart hook directory creation must keep the `ai_dev_loop` XDG state root and managed subdirectories user-only.

These regressions came from Phase 7.5 staged reviews and must remain covered when desktop integration or bridge code changes:

- bridge status must compare `sessions/from-desktop` with the resolved Windows desktop sessions directory instead of accepting any symlink;
- bridge listing must refuse mispointed symlinks;
- bridge removal must work using WSL-side paths even when Windows home detection fails;
- desktop hook command generation must validate and quote every dynamic token;
- tests must remain hermetic when WSL inherits DrvFS `TMP`, `TEMP`, `TMPDIR`, or `CODEX_HOME`;
- legacy shell bridge helpers must not replace the Python CLI as the product surface.

## Real Install

- Real installation into the user's HOME is allowed only after the active phase plan explicitly approves it and automated validation has passed.
- If real installation encounters invalid or ambiguous user files, stop safely and report remediation.
- Do not bypass `/hooks` trust during real install.
